\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Counting-basis realizations of background transport}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Choose orderings of #{V,E,V',E'} and use the coefficient maps
#{C_Vf=(f(v_i))_i}, #{C_Ea=(a(e_j))_j}, and their primed versions.
These send the indicator bases to the standard bases. Write
#{D=C_E\extd_{\mathbb R}C_V^{-1}}, #{P=C_{V'}P_{\mathrm{op}}C_V^{-1}} and
#{J=C_{E'}J_{\mathrm{op}}C_E^{-1}}, and use #{W,L,Q} for the
corresponding endomorphism arrays. The intrinsic identities of
\ref{fdip-003D} and \ref{fdip-00KC} become
##{L=-D^TWD,\qquad P^{-1}=P^T,\qquad J^{-1}=J^T,
\qquad D'=JDP^T,\qquad W'=JWJ^T.}
The array of the split component #{B:Y\to X} is distinct from the
incidence array #{D}. Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.reindexLinearEquiv}
and \leanref{Matrix.reindexRingEquiv} support these coordinate
reindexings; they supply neither endpoint data nor perfect pairings
on arbitrary modules.}

\p{Two broader comparisons retain less data. The
[positive-scaling Laplacian relation](fdip-003H) forgets mass, state
and partition and compares nonzero Laplacians by #{L'=cPLP^{-1}}
with #{c>0} and a vertex permutation. The
[cycle trace ratio](fdip-000I) still separates two examples.
For a general invertible real field map, use the same coefficient
maps and write #{T} for its array. Setting #{y=T\phi} gives
precision array #{T^{-T}QT^{-1}} and transported counting pairing
#{y^TT^{-T}T^{-1}z}; the reference Jacobian is
#{\extd y=|\det T|\,\extd\phi}. These are the declared-basis formulas in
\ref{fdip-00KH}. [Jacobian compensation](fdip-003M) transports the
normalized law, although the [quadratic-isometry example](fdip-000Z)
loses the reference pairing or retained projection. Such a map need
not be an exact or signed background arrow.}

\p{The coordinate density convention is
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}; its
covariance parameter equals inverse precision by \ref{fdip-000B}.}
